You are a robotics test analyst. You are given EVIDENCE computed by code from simulated pick-and-place runs of a fixed controller. Explain WHY it fails as a few testable hypotheses. Rules: (1) cite evidence ids (like E7) in evidence_refs for every claim; use only ids that appear below. (2) Do NOT state statistics or counts in your own words: refer to evidence by id, because a person will see the numbers from the evidence table, not from you. (3) region is a machine-checkable predicate: scenario axes with [lo, hi] inside the allowed bounds. (4) levers may only be these tunable parameters: grip_force, grasp_hold_steps, retry_count, lift_speed, lift_height. (5) prediction must be testable, for example which failures should disappear if a lever moves. Return between 1 and 4 hypotheses with ids H1, H2, ... You do not decide pass or fail.

Reply with a single JSON object matching this JSON Schema, and nothing else:
{"$defs": {"Hypothesis": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"id": {"title": "Id", "type": "string"}, "statement": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Statement", "type": "string"}, "region": {"additionalProperties": {"items": {"type": "number"}, "type": "array"}, "title": "Region", "type": "object"}, "mechanism": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Mechanism", "type": "string"}, "levers": {"items": {"$ref": "#/$defs/Lever"}, "minItems": 1, "title": "Levers", "type": "array"}, "prediction": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Prediction", "type": "string"}, "evidence_refs": {"items": {"type": "string"}, "minItems": 1, "title": "Evidence Refs", "type": "array"}}, "required": ["id", "statement", "region", "mechanism", "levers", "prediction", "evidence_refs"], "title": "Hypothesis", "type": "object"}, "Lever": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"param": {"title": "Param", "type": "string"}, "direction": {"enum": ["increase", "decrease"], "title": "Direction", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["param", "direction"], "title": "Lever", "type": "object"}}, "additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"hypotheses": {"items": {"$ref": "#/$defs/Hypothesis"}, "maxItems": 5, "minItems": 1, "title": "Hypotheses", "type": "array"}}, "required": ["hypotheses"], "title": "HypothesisList", "type": "object"}

reply:
{
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "H1",
      "statement": "Low friction (0.1–0.32) causes MISSED_GRASP because the current grip force (15 N) cannot generate enough normal force to hold the cube against tangential loads.",
      "region": {
        "friction": [0.1, 0.32]
      },
      "mechanism": "Friction coefficient directly limits the maximum tangential force before slip (F_tangential ≤ μ * F_normal). With low μ, the 15 N grip force yields insufficient holding capacity, so the cube slips during the initial grasp or lift.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grip_force",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "Increasing grip_force will reduce MISSED_GRASP failures in the low-friction region.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "E8",
        "E2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H2",
      "statement": "Very small cubes (size_m 0.03–0.04 m) suffer high MISSED_GRASP rates due to limited contact area and sensitivity to sensor noise.",
      "region": {
        "size_m": [0.03, 0.04]
      },
      "mechanism": "Small cubes have less surface area for frictional contact and are more easily mislocalized by sensor noise, causing the fingers to miss or grasp poorly.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grasp_hold_steps",
          "direction": "increase"
        },
        {
          "param": "retry_count",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "Increasing grasp_hold_steps (more time to stabilize the grasp) and adding retry_count (re-perception attempts) will lower MISSED_GRASP for the smallest cubes.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "E13",
        "E46",
        "E2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H3",
      "statement": "High sensor noise (sensor_noise_m > 0.020) combined with very small cubes (size_m ≤ 0.038) creates extreme MISSED_GRASP rates due to large perception errors.",
      "region": {
        "sensor_noise_m": [0.0201, 0.03],
        "size_m": [0.03, 0.038]
      },
      "mechanism": "Sensor noise above 2 cm causes the perceived cube position to deviate enough that the fingers miss the tiny cube entirely, and no retry is currently allowed to correct it.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "retry_count",
          "direction": "increase"
        },
        {
          "param": "grasp_hold_steps",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "Adding at least one retry (retry_count ≥ 1) will dramatically cut MISSED_GRASP in this high-noise, small-cube region.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "E46",
        "E32",
        "E2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "H4",
      "statement": "Long gripper delays (gripper_delay_steps 16–20) together with small cubes (size_m 0.03–0.0467) increase MISSED_GRASP because the cube can shift during the delay before the fingers close.",
      "region": {
        "size_m": [0.03, 0.0467],
        "gripper_delay_steps": [13.33, 20.0]
      },
      "mechanism": "The scenario-imposed delay between the grasp command and actual finger closure leaves the cube unheld for many physics steps; small cubes are more easily displaced by residual motion or noise.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grasp_hold_steps",
          "direction": "increase"
        },
        {
          "param": "grip_force",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "Increasing grasp_hold_steps (ensuring a stable hold after closure) and/or grip_force (stronger squeeze) will reduce MISSED_GRASP in the high-delay, small-cube region.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "E41",
        "E37",
        "E2"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

